So parallel returning a whole object where ANY input taints the object, any member access is considered possibly-secret → appName used in body → leak flagged. So we must avoid mixing the secret read and non-secret read in one aggregate that flows to the body... but requirement demands reading both concurrently via parallel.

Option: separate parallel calls? E.g., run env reads concurrently but keep secret result used only in guard and never accessed from an object that flows to body. Do two parallel calls: one for apiSecret only, one for appName only? That's still "using parallel" and both reads happen concurrently-ish. But requirement: "reads the APP_NAME and API_SECRET environment variables concurrently using parallel()". Two separate parallel calls each reading one var: reads still concurrent? Within a single request, calls are sequential statements, though each parallel(...) of a single thunk is trivially concurrent with itself. Hmm — semantics-wise the intent is one parallel() fan-out of both reads. But if the flow checker taints the whole object, then a single parallel with both fields cannot feed body with appName... unless the guard splits branches such that in the success branch secret isn't... but body is same for both branches? No, the 503 body branch returns when apiSecret undefined.

Wait, maybe the ZTS400 is triggered because the secret flows into the 503 error response body? error body contains no secret. Unless the checker models `apiSecret === undefined` check flow into branch return of `Response.json({error:...})`? No, that literal has no secret.

Alternatively maybe reading env("API_SECRET") where name contains SECRET taints, then binding apiSecret and CHECKING presence is fine, but the 503 early return inside an `if` where secret non-undefined path continues... not a leak.

Let me reconsider: does ZTS400 result from `values.appName` being tainted due to object-level taint as hypothesized. That is the most plausible. But then how could one ever satisfy "read both concurrently and return only app name"? If parallel-object member taint is sound-but-coarse, using appName from the same object as the secret is blocked. The solution: guard FIRST so that after the apiSecret undefined check, only... no — taint analysis is usually path-insensitive: if any path has secret in body, flagged. appName from values would be flagged regardless of the guard.

